/* Single source of truth for the whole site.

   Times are seconds. Cost, admissions and CS-program fields come from the U.S.
   Department of Education College Scorecard (api.data.gov), matched school by
   school against an explicit name map — no fuzzy matching, because fuzzy matching
   produced confident false positives (Georgia State -> Georgia Tech and six others).

   Cross country data is transcribed from 2025 championship result pages on TFRRS.
   `xc.slot` is where he would have finished inside that team's scoring seven,
   averaged over every race of theirs on file; `xc.v7` is his projection minus
   their 7th man, so negative means inside the seven. See methodology.html §3.

   Detail data — every meet each team travelled to, the named top sevens, and
   venue coordinates — lives in detail.js, loaded only by school.html.

   Candidate list: every NCAA school in any division inside each ring that sponsors
   men's cross country, plus the NAIA programs the sweep happened to find. The rings are
   300 driving miles of Greenville SC, 20 driving miles of midtown Manhattan *plus all of
   Long Island*, and 20 driving miles of the Chicago Loop. Sponsorship was checked
   conference by conference from each conference's sponsored-sports table, so a school
   missing here is a school whose conference table did not list men's cross country.

   A Division-1-only rule was applied to this file and then reversed. It archived 96 D2,
   D3 and NAIA schools in a separate NOT_D1 array; they are back in SCHOOLS, REMOVED,
   NO_TRACK and NO_PROGRAM where they belong, with measurements, tiers, coaches and
   reasoning untouched, and the `was` tag that recorded where each one sat is gone.
   Division is now a column and a filter, not a gate: D2 is where partial athletic aid
   actually lives, and thirteen of the schools the rule removed were at target tier.
   See methodology.html §6a.

   Greenville mileages are real road distance from the search center, returned by OSRM
   driving directions and rounded to the mile — not straight-line estimates. An earlier
   version of this file scaled great-circle distance by 1.18 (1.35 in the mountains) and
   was off by as much as 54 miles, which mattered at the edge of the ring. */

/* His projections. The 1600 is a real mark improved by one more track season
   (4:21 today -> 4:18); the 1500 is that projected 1600 converted with Riegel at
   e = 1.06, factor (1500/1600)^1.06 = 0.9339, so 258s -> 240.9s. The 5000 stays
   3200-anchored (9:29 -> 3000 8:53 -> 15:20) — see methodology.html §1 and §1a.

   proj8k and proj10k now come off a *real* mark, not a projected one: his 5K cross
   country best of 15:40 (940s). That is the second time this anchor has improved on a
   mark he actually ran - ~15:59 projected, then 15:55, now 15:40 - and each time the
   whole cross country chain is rescaled rather than adjusted by hand.
   Each distance gets its own projection and a race is only ever compared against the
   one for the distance it was actually run at:
     8K  = 940 * 1.6340 = 1536 (25:36)
     10K = 940 * 2^1.06 = 1960 (32:40)
   The 10K used to be 8K * 1.26 and that factor was too small (it put the 10K 25s fast).
   Both anchors have been measured against the 1,252 team-seasons in the 2025-26 sweep,
   comparing each team's own nth man at the two distances in the same season:
     5K -> 8K   measured 1.620-1.639 by slot (248 team-seasons, 1,608 slot pairs),
                so 1.6340 and 26:00 stand as they are.
     8K -> 10K  measured 1.266 at a team's #1, rising to 1.288 at its 7th, median
                1.272 (208 team-seasons). 1.26 is below the whole range. Riegel at
                e = 1.06 predicts 1.267 and the front of a field matches it almost
                exactly, which is why the 10K is scaled straight off the 5K mark
                like the 8K rather than off the 8K's own projection.
   A team's 7th man fades harder at 10K than its #1 does (1.288 vs 1.266) — the back of
   a scoring seven is in traffic at a regional with the team score already settled. That
   is a fact about their 7th man, not about him, so it is documented in §3c rather than
   built into his projection. Nothing projects the 15:40 forward — §1c has the sensitivity
   if you want to, and its 15:40 row is what just happened. The 15:40 also implies about
   15:01 on the track via the ~39s grass-to-track differential, 19 seconds faster than the
   3200-anchored 15:20 this file still carries: 15:20 is a floor, not a goal, and the
   board will not stack a projection on a projection to say otherwise.

   Every slot/g1/v7/vlast/spread number in this file and in detail.js is generated
   from the raw runner times, with each meet's course correction applied. Do not hand
   edit them: change ATHLETE or the times, then regenerate, or they will drift out of
   agreement the way the South Atlantic correction did (methodology.html §3b). */
const ATHLETE = {
  proj1600: 258, proj1600Label: '4:18',
  proj1500: 241, proj1500Label: '4:01',
  proj5000: 920, proj5000Label: '15:20', band: [915, 925],
  proj8k: 1536, proj8kLabel: '25:36',
  proj10k: 1960, proj10kLabel: '32:40',
  /* Cross country 5Ks need no projection at all: 15:40 is the mark itself, the one
     the 8K and 10K above are scaled from. Early-season meets run a lot of them. */
  proj5kxc: 940, proj5kxcLabel: '15:40',
};

const TIERS = {
  target:  { label: 'Target',  glyph: '\u25b2', desc: 'Inside their scoring seven \u2014 he would slot in around 4th to 7th man' },
  deep:    { label: 'Deep',    glyph: '\u25a0', desc: 'Just outside the seven \u2014 strong program, but he would not travel as a freshman' },
  verify:  { label: 'Verify',  glyph: '\u25c6', desc: 'No cross country data yet \u2014 unmeasured rather than borderline, get the real numbers' },
  caution: { label: 'Caution', glyph: '\u25bc', desc: 'He would arrive at or near their #1 \u2014 thin training group' },
};

/* Course offsets applied before comparing his projection to a team's times.
   Derived only where three or more teams ran both the meet and their regional,
   so the offset is a property of the course rather than of one team's bad day. */
const COURSE_NOTES = {
  'South Atlantic Conference XC Championship':
    'ran about 96 seconds slow at 8K \u2014 Anderson, Catawba and Wingate each confirm it against the D2 regional',
  'CCIW Championships':
    'ran about 31 seconds fast at 8K, from four teams cross-checked against the D3 Midwest regional',
};

